---
name: ptt-take
description: "Take a role or task for this session; conflicts are refused. Arguments: designer (task T0000), integrator, a task ID or name, or none to list ready tasks."
disable-model-invocation: true
---

# Take a role or a task

Taking is the user's choice: only this command, typed in the session that
takes, approves recording it (an AtE does not); the work itself needs AtE
(rules file).

1. No argument: `<records>/bin/ptt tasks`; show the `ready` tasks and ask
   which one. Stop.
2. `<records>/bin/ptt take <designer|integrator|task>` (`designer` is
   `T0000`; a task by ID `T0007`, `7`, `T0007.1`, or name `wall-distance`).
   If refused (held, draft, blocked), report why and stop; never work around
   it.
3. The tool prints your holder name (`designer-g2`, `integrator-1`,
   `track-T0007`). It is your name from now on: `--as NAME` (the NAME
   argument for `leave`, `resume`, `whoami`, and `inbox`; optional for `record` and `pr-move`,
   added to the commit message), and `session:` in request messages. The
   tool binds it to this session where the agent tool gives a session ID;
   one session holds one role or task. In Claude Code, `/clear` starts a
   fresh session ID: continue with `/ptt-resume <name>`. In Codex, a fresh
   chat or fork continues with `$ptt-resume <name>`. Continue by resuming,
   not by taking again. A different session ID cannot write as the bound
   holder until it has resumed; continuing the same ID keeps the binding.
   The tool also prints the session's name (`session name:`) and the line
   that renames the session: pass it on to the user with its reason (in
   Claude Code, automatic messages reach this session only by that name);
   the name is recorded, so never invent or change it.
4. Read what you start from, then report what you hold, your supervisor,
   the next step, and every `note:` the tool printed:
   - integrator: `<records>/handoff/integrator.md`, `<records>/bin/pr list`;
   - a task (also `designer`): `<records>/bin/ptt show <N>` (charter,
     amendments, previous holder's `status.md`), then `<records>/bin/ptt
     inbox <name>` for what waits on you. A `design` task makes you the
     designer of its scope: you make and close its subtasks; `T0000`'s
     holder makes the top-level tasks.
     After acting on an amendment, use `<records>/bin/ptt adopt TASK N[,N]
     --as NAME` on the user's AtE; if you disagree, write a note to the designer.

   Then go on by yourself, read-only (rules file): writing files, making
   worktrees, and runs that create run records wait for AtE.
   - A task holder (worker, track): read every item of the charter's "Read
     first" and its amendments, inspect the code and tests they name, and
     present a plan: the commits, what each changes, the tests, risks and
     Unknowns; end with a decision list asking for AtE.
   - The integrator: `<records>/bin/pr list` and `<records>/bin/pr gc`
     (read-only); list the requests waiting for review and ask which to
     review first.
   - A designer: its inbox; list what waits on it (done reports, leave
     requests, notes).
5. If the tool notes that `T0000` is not filled in (a project just set up,
   or a placeholder left since), find what exists: earlier handover files
   in `<records>/handoff/` (not `integrator.md`, `notes/`), "superseded"
   lines in `partitur-install-report.md` in the workspace root, the design
   document, earlier briefs. Propose one plan: fill in the charter's
   placeholders in place (the first fill-in is a direct edit, not an
   amendment), move the earlier handover into `status.md`, record both (on
   the user's AtE); then a draft task per briefed piece of work, each by
   the user's `/ptt-mktask`, naming the brief as its source.
6. While holding a task, record `status.md` (`<records>/bin/ptt record`) at
   least once a day of work, also when the work is on a code branch: a
   supervisor treats seven days without a record as possibly gone.
